// PARKED REPRO (borrow-safety gap -- mutability compat NOT enforced). `handle`
// takes `c: &mut Counter` (it may mutate through it), but `main` passes an
// IMMUTABLE reference `&self.c`. This compiles at --check with no error: the
// caller lent only immutable access, yet the callee holds a `&mut` and may write
// through it. A borrow-rule violation that goes unenforced.
//
// PRECISE LOCATION: `validate_call_arguments_handles` (omega-validation/calls.rs)
// handles a mutability mismatch by `continue`-ing (it SKIPS the argument's checks)
// when `parameter.is_mutable && !is_mutable`, rather than pushing a diagnostic. The
// SAFE direction (`&mut x` for a `&` parameter) is correctly accepted; only the
// unsafe direction (immutable arg -> `&mut` param) needs rejecting.
//
// This is a facet of the KNOWN "ownership enforcement is frontend-modeled but not
// enforced" subsystem (see TASKS + memory drop-bodies-not-executed: drop bodies
// don't run, use-after-move isn't rejected). Fence-vs-implement is Zach's call --
// converting the `continue` to an error changes language surface (code may rely on
// the laxity), so it is NOT fenced unilaterally. It belongs to the eventual move/
// borrow checker. Memory-safe today (value semantics + drop-is-no-op).
//
// FIX SKETCH -- NOT a one-liner (corrected 2026-07-04 after analysis): the
// tempting "replace the `continue` with a diagnostic" is WRONG. `is_mutable` at
// that site is a SYNTACTIC check -- `matches!(arg, ExpressionNode::Mutable(_))`,
// i.e. "is the argument literally `&mut ...` syntax". A valid `&mut` FORWARD --
// `state a(x: &mut Counter) { transition { _ -> b(x) } }` passing `x` onward to a
// `&mut Counter` param -- has `arg = Name(x)`, which is NOT a Mutable node, so
// is_mutable(x) = FALSE. Erroring on `parameter.is_mutable && !is_mutable` would
// FALSE-POSITIVE on that forwarding (verified: the forward compiles today via the
// `continue`). So the real check needs SEMANTIC mutability: resolve the argument's
// declared reference mutability (via declared_place_type_raw -> is the base a
// `&mut`?) and reject only when the arg is provably an IMMUTABLE borrow of a place
// for a `&mut` parameter. That is borrow-checker work (mutability tracking through
// bindings), consistent with the ownership-enforcement subsystem being deferred.

boundary trait Console { machine exit_process(return_code: i32); }

data Counter { n: i32 in Wrapping; }
data Main { console: Console; c: Counter; }

machine Main::main(&mut self) {
    transition { _ -> handle(&self.c) } // &self.c (immutable) for a `&mut Counter` param
    state handle(&mut self, c: &mut Counter) {
        c.n = c.n + 1; // mutates through a borrow the caller lent immutably
        self.console.exit_process(c.n);
    }
}
